Think of yourself as an orchestra conductor, a dungeon master, and a jazz musician simultaneously.

The conductor creates coherence. The dungeon master creates a world in which other people have agency. The jazz musician listens, improvises, and knows when to play or leave space.

The objective isn’t to make everyone follow your story. It’s to help multiple stories become greater through their interaction.

1. The Ensemble Engine

An ensemble is a network of distinct capacities, not a hierarchy of main characters and extras. The center belongs to the shared purpose, not one individual.

There are six fundamental orchestration moves.

Move	What you do
Recognize	Discover what makes each person come alive
Invite	Offer meaningful roles without prescribing identities
Connect	Bring complementary people, skills, and ambitions together
Amplify	Make their strengths visible to others
Harmonize	Coordinate different needs without erasing differences
Release	Let others lead, improvise, and own the result

The sixth is particularly important. If people cannot succeed without your direction, you’ve built dependency, not an ensemble.

2. Learn to conduct attention

Attention is one of the most powerful currencies in human interaction.

You can steer it toward yourself, distribute it, or use it to create relationships between people.

Consider three approaches to a gathering:

The performer

Entertains the group, tells great stories, commands attention. People remember you.

The facilitator

Asks good questions, invites quieter voices, keeps the conversation moving. People feel included.

The orchestrator

Recognizes hidden complementarities, introduces people through their shared interests, helps them create something together. People remember each other.

You should be able to move between all three.

For example, someone mentions an interest in photography, and another mentions wanting to document a family trip.

Instead of merely talking about photography, you connect their interests:

“Wait, you two should talk. You’re both working on different sides of the same idea.”

Then withdraw from the center of that interaction.

You’ve created a potentially lasting relationship that didn’t depend on keeping you in the spotlight.

