6. Know when not to conduct

There’s a paradox at the center of all this.

The better you become at organizing an ensemble, the easier it is to accidentally become its controlling intelligence.

You might begin managing everyone’s experiences, engineering every relationship, or feeling responsible for everybody’s happiness.

That is main character syndrome wearing the costume of benevolent leadership.

The antidote is distributed authorship: everyone retains the right to surprise you, disagree, decline the invitation, or pursue a different story.

And remember, you are allowed to be cared for, surprised, and led by other people too.

A magnificent ensemble isn’t one in which every instrument obeys the conductor.

It’s one in which the musicians learn to hear each other so well that beautiful things happen even when the conductor puts down the baton.

The highest form of orchestration is creating the conditions for other people’s agency to multiply—including your own.

That’s how you stop merely being the protagonist and become a builder of worlds.